//? Title: Test for an ordered trend across groups
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare readings at three ordered settings

Three independent groups supply readings at low, medium, and high
settings. The increasing alternative was specified before comparing
them. For every pair from different settings, the Jonckheere–Terpstra
statistic awards a point when the higher setting has the higher reading
and half a point for a tie. This example uses a normal reference that
accounts for the group sizes and ties among the pooled readings.

```{cpp, id=ordered-group-trend, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/nonparametric.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<std::vector<double>> readings{{0, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3, 4}, {2, 4, 5}};
    const auto test = jonckheere_terpstra_test(readings, Alternative::Greater);
    std::println("The higher setting earns {:g} points across {:.0f} pairs, "
                 "or {:.1f}% of the possible points. The increasing-trend p-value is {:.4f}.",
                 test.statistic, test.parameter2, 100 * test.estimate1, test.p_value);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The higher setting earns 29 points across 33 pairs, or 87.9% of the possible points. The increasing-trend p-value is 0.0073.
// result_end

[Jonckheere’s ordered-alternative test|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/41.1-2.133]
